</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-ftli">
<title>Line Integrals in Conservative Fields</title>

<p>
Next, we will study the line integrals of conservative fields. As you
will recognize, if you think of the gradient of a vector field as its
derivative, then the fundamental theorem of line integrals makes a
similar statement for vector-valued functions as the one made for
definite integrals of scalar-valued functions, i.e., the one that you
learned in MATH 12 and you know as FTC II.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-ftli">
<title>Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>C</m> be a smooth curve joining the point <m>A</m> to the point
<m>B</m> in the plane or in space and parametrized by
<m>\mathbf r(t)</m>. Let <m>f</m> be a differentiable function with a
continuous gradient vector <m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m> on a domain
<m>D</m> containing <m>C</m>. Then
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-ftli">
\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = f(B) - f(A).
</men>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>
Assuming that the curve <m>C</m> is parametrised as
<m>\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\mathbf i + y(t)\mathbf j + z(t)\mathbf k</m>, we
have
<m>\mathbf v(t) = \dfrac{d\mathbf r}{dt}
= \dfrac{dx}{dt}\mathbf i + \dfrac{dy}{dt}\mathbf j
+ \dfrac{dz}{dt}\mathbf k</m>
and we can rewrite the line integral as
</p>

<md xml:id="eq-ftli-proof">
<mrow>
\int_C \mathbf F(x(t),y(t),z(t))\cdot d\mathbf r
\amp= \int_a^b \nabla f\cdot\frac{d\mathbf r}{dt}\,dt
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_a^b
\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\mathbf i
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\mathbf j
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\mathbf k\right)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\qquad \cdot
\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\mathbf i + \frac{dy}{dt}\mathbf j
+ \frac{dz}{dt}\mathbf k\right)dt
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_a^b
\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\frac{dz}{dt}\right)dt
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_a^b \frac{df}{dt}\,dt
= \int_a^b df = f(b) - f(a).
</mrow>
</md>
</proof>
</theorem>

<p>
In <xref ref="fig-ftli-region-tikz"/>, the boundary of the region
<m>D</m> is marked as dotted and the curve <m>C</m> is in red. According
to the fundamental theorem, the line integral of a conservative vector
field <m>\mathbf F</m> can be easily calculated by calculating the
values of its corresponding potential function <m>f</m> at the beginning
(point <m>A</m>) and end (point <m>B</m>) of the curve <m>C</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ftli-region-tikz">
<caption>
A smooth curve <m>C</m> joining <m>A(x_1,y_1,z_1)</m> to
<m>B(x_2,y_2,z_2)</m> inside an open region <m>D</m> in space, whose
boundary is drawn dotted. For a conservative field
<m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m>, the line integral along <m>C</m> equals
<m>f(B) - f(A)</m>, no matter how <m>C</m> winds from <m>A</m> to
<m>B</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a dotted oval marking the boundary
of a region D. Inside the region, a red curve C winds from the point A
with coordinates x 1 comma y 1 comma z 1 to the point B with
coordinates x 2 comma y 2 comma z 2.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{20},
      width=12cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-0.4, xmax=2.6, ymin=-0.5, ymax=2.9, zmin=-0.2, zmax=2.6,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- dotted boundary of the region D (a tilted closed curve) ----
    \addplot3[black, dotted, line width=1.3pt, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1.15 + 1.35*cos(x)}, {1.3 + 1.55*sin(x)},
       {1.15 + 0.45*sin(x) + 0.35*cos(2*x)});
    \node[anchor=south, font=\large] at (axis cs:1.6,2.3,0.55) {$D$};
    % ---- the curve C from A to B (a gentle S-curve, in red/vermillion) ----
    \addplot3[oivermillion, line width=1.7pt, -{Latex[length=2.2mm]},
        domain=0:1, samples=81, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({0.9 + 0.5*x + 0.45*sin(360*x)},
       {0.15 + 2.45*x},
       {0.6 + 1.1*x});
    \node[oivermillion, anchor=south, font=\large]
      at (axis cs:1.05,1.5,1.45) {$C$};
    % ---- endpoints ----
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, color=oiblue,
        forget plot] coordinates {(0.9,0.15,0.6) (1.4,2.6,1.7)};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.9,0.15,0.4)
      {$A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$};
    \node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.42,2.62,1.78)
      {$B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The central idea of the theorem<mdash/>that only the endpoints
matter<mdash/>is animated in <xref ref="fig-ftli-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ftli-video">
<caption>
Animation of the fundamental theorem of line integrals for the field
<m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m> with
<m>f(x,y,z) = 1/\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}</m>. Two different curves join
<m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>: the helix <m>C_1</m> and the
straight segment <m>C_2</m>. The accumulated value of each line
integral is tracked as the paths are traced, and both integrals arrive
at the same value <m>f(B) - f(A) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+4\pi^2}} - 1</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-ftli-path-independence"
       source="videos/ftli_path_independence_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
In the next example, we will see how we can use the above theorem.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-ftli-helix">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the scalar-valued function
<m>f(x,y,z) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Calculate the gradient vector field
<m>\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \nabla f</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate the line integral of the vector field
<m>\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \nabla f</m> over the curve <m>C_1</m>
parametrized as
<m>\mathbf r(t) = \cos(t)\mathbf i + \sin(t)\mathbf j + t\mathbf k</m>
from <m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What is the result of <m>\int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r</m> from
<m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m> where <m>C_2</m> is the line
connecting the two points?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
<em>A.</em> We compute the three partial derivatives of
<m>f(x,y,z) = \left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{-1/2}</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \nabla f
\amp= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\mathbf i
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\mathbf j
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\mathbf k
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= -\frac{x}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}\,\mathbf i
- \frac{y}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}\,\mathbf j
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\quad - \frac{z}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}\,\mathbf k.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
<em>B.</em> Of course, one way would be to calculate the line integral
using the vector field calculated in part A (as we used to do); however,
<xref ref="thm-ftli"/> provides us with a much more efficient way of
calculating the line integral as follows:
</p>

<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-ftli-helix-value" number="yes">
\int_{C_1} \mathbf F(x(t),y(t),z(t))\cdot d\mathbf r
\amp= f(1,0,2\pi) - f(1,0,0)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + 4\pi^2}} - 1.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
<em>C.</em> The result is the same as part B, since the vector field
<m>\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \nabla f</m> is a conservative field, which means
that the result of the line integral is independent of the chosen path.
Note that the line integral is calculated from <m>(1,0,0)</m> to
<m>(1,0,2\pi)</m> in both cases. The two curves <m>C_1</m> and
<m>C_2</m> are shown in <xref ref="fig-helix-segment-tikz"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-helix-segment-tikz">
<caption>
The two paths of <xref ref="example-ftli-helix"/> joining
<m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>: the helix <m>C_1</m>, parametrized
by <m>\mathbf r(t) = \cos(t)\mathbf i + \sin(t)\mathbf j + t\mathbf k</m>,
and the straight segment <m>C_2</m>. Since <m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m>
is conservative, both line integrals equal
<m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+4\pi^2}} - 1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space showing a blue helix that winds once
around the z axis, climbing from the point one comma zero comma zero up
to the point one comma zero comma two pi, and a vertical vermillion
segment joining the same two points directly. Both endpoints are
marked and labeled.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{16},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-1.4, xmax=1.5, ymin=-1.5, ymax=1.5, zmin=0, zmax=7.2,
      clip=false,
      legend style={
        at={(1.02,1.04)}, anchor=north east,
        font=\small,
        draw=black!35, rounded corners=2pt,
        fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
        inner sep=6pt, row sep=3pt,
      },
      legend cell align=left,
      legend image post style={line width=1.6pt, sharp plot},
    ]
    % ---- helix C1: (cos t, sin t, t), t in [0, 2pi] ----
    \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.7pt, -{Latex[length=2.2mm]},
        domain=0:360, samples=121, samples y=0]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {2*pi*x/360});
    \addlegendentry{$C_1$: helix $\mathbf r(t)=\cos t\,\mathbf i+\sin t\,\mathbf j+t\,\mathbf k$}
    % ---- segment C2 from (1,0,0) to (1,0,2pi) ----
    \addplot3[oivermillion, line width=1.7pt, -{Latex[length=2.2mm]},
        samples y=0] coordinates {(1,0,0) (1,0,6.28318)};
    \addlegendentry{$C_2$: straight segment}
    % ---- dashed guide circle in the xy-plane ----
    \addplot3[black!30, dashed, line width=0.6pt, domain=0:360,
        samples=97, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, 0);
    % ---- endpoints ----
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1,0,0) (1,0,6.28318)};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.06,0,-0.35)
      {$(1,0,0)$};
    \node[oigreen, anchor=south west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:1.02,0,6.5) {$(1,0,2\pi)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-potential-function">
<title>Finding a Potential Function</title>

<example xml:id="example-potential-function">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the vector field
<m>\mathbf F = yz\,\mathbf i + xz\,\mathbf j + (xy + 2z)\mathbf k</m>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a function <m>f</m> such that <m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Use the solution of part (A) to calculate the line integral from
<m>(1,0,-2)</m> to <m>(4,6,3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
<em>A.</em> Writing
<m>\mathbf F = yz\,\mathbf i + xz\,\mathbf j + (xy+2z)\mathbf k
= \left\langle \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x},\,
\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y},\, \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial z}
\right\rangle</m>,
we match the components one at a time; the three steps are summarized
in <xref ref="fig-potential-steps-prefigure"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Step I.</em> Since <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x} = yz</m>, we
integrate with respect to <m>x</m> (here <m>x</m> is the variable and
<m>y</m> and <m>z</m> are constants), so
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-potential-step1">
f(x,y,z) = xyz + g(y,z),
</men>

<p>
where the constant of integration <m>g(y,z)</m> is allowed to depend on
<m>y</m> and <m>z</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Step II.</em> Since <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y} = xz</m>, we
differentiate <xref ref="eq-potential-step1"/> with respect to
<m>y</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\Big(xyz + g(y,z)\Big)
= xz + \frac{\partial g}{\partial y} = xz
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\frac{\partial g}{\partial y} = 0,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
so <m>g</m> does <em>not</em> depend on <m>y</m>, and we can write
<m>g(y,z) = h(z)</m>; that is,
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-potential-step2">
f(x,y,z) = xyz + h(z).
</men>

<p>
<em>Step III.</em> Since <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial z} = xy + 2z</m>,
we differentiate <xref ref="eq-potential-step2"/> with respect to
<m>z</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial}{\partial z}\Big(xyz + h(z)\Big)
\amp= xy + \frac{dh}{dz} = xy + 2z
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\Rightarrow\quad \frac{dh}{dz} = 2z
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
h(z) = z^2 + C.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Putting the three steps together, we conclude
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-potential-answer">
f(x,y,z) = xyz + z^2 + C.
</men>

<p>
<em>B.</em> By <xref ref="thm-ftli"/>, with <m>A(1,0,-2)</m> and
<m>B(4,6,3)</m>,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
\amp= f(4,6,3) - f(1,0,-2)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \big((4)(6)(3) + 3^2\big) - \big((1)(0)(-2) + (-2)^2\big)
= 81 - 4 = 77.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-potential-steps-prefigure">
<caption>
The three-step cascade for building the potential function of
<m>\mathbf F = yz\,\mathbf i + xz\,\mathbf j + (xy+2z)\mathbf k</m>.
Each component of <m>\mathbf F</m> pins down one more piece of
<m>f</m>, shrinking the unknown <q>constant of integration</q> from
<m>g(y,z)</m> to <m>h(z)</m> to the true constant <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-potential-steps">
<diagram dimensions="(460,300)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(0,0,10,7.2)">
<rectangle at="ps-box1" lower-left="(0.4,5.4)" dimensions="(4.1,1.4)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="#dbeaf7" thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-eq1" anchor="(2.45,6.1)" alignment="c">
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x} = yz</m>
</label>
<vector at="ps-arrow1" v="(2.4,0)" tail="(4.7,6.1)" stroke="#009E73"
    thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-arrow1-label" anchor="(5.9,6.35)" alignment="n"
    color="#009E73">integrate in <m>x</m></label>
<rectangle at="ps-box1b" lower-left="(7.3,5.4)" dimensions="(2.4,1.4)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="white" thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-eq1b" anchor="(8.5,6.1)" alignment="c">
<m>xyz + g(y,z)</m>
</label>
<rectangle at="ps-box2" lower-left="(0.4,3.1)" dimensions="(4.1,1.4)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="#dbeaf7" thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-eq2" anchor="(2.45,3.8)" alignment="c">
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y} = xz</m>
</label>
<vector at="ps-arrow2" v="(2.4,0)" tail="(4.7,3.8)" stroke="#009E73"
    thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-arrow2-label" anchor="(5.9,4.05)" alignment="n"
    color="#009E73"><m>\partial g/\partial y = 0</m></label>
<rectangle at="ps-box2b" lower-left="(7.3,3.1)" dimensions="(2.4,1.4)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="white" thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-eq2b" anchor="(8.5,3.8)" alignment="c">
<m>xyz + h(z)</m>
</label>
<rectangle at="ps-box3" lower-left="(0.4,0.8)" dimensions="(4.1,1.4)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="#dbeaf7" thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-eq3" anchor="(2.45,1.5)" alignment="c">
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial z} = xy + 2z</m>
</label>
<vector at="ps-arrow3" v="(2.4,0)" tail="(4.7,1.5)" stroke="#009E73"
    thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-arrow3-label" anchor="(5.9,1.75)" alignment="n"
    color="#009E73"><m>h'(z) = 2z</m></label>
<rectangle at="ps-box3b" lower-left="(7.3,0.8)" dimensions="(2.4,1.4)"
    stroke="#D55E00" fill="#fbe3d0" thickness="2"/>
<label at="ps-eq3b" anchor="(8.5,1.5)" alignment="c">
<m>xyz + z^2 + C</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A flow diagram with three rows, one for each component of the vector field. Each row starts with a partial derivative condition in a blue box and ends with an updated formula for f. Row one: partial f partial x equals y z leads, by integrating in x, to x y z plus g of y and z. Row two: partial f partial y equals x z forces partial g partial y equals zero, giving x y z plus h of z. Row three: partial f partial z equals x y plus 2 z forces h prime of z equals 2 z, giving the final answer x y z plus z squared plus C in a vermillion box.">
  <annotation ref="ps-box1" text="Step one. The x component of F says partial f partial x equals y z."/>
  <annotation ref="ps-box1b" text="Integrating in x gives f equals x y z plus g of y and z, where the constant of integration may depend on y and z."/>
  <annotation ref="ps-box2" text="Step two. The y component of F says partial f partial y equals x z."/>
  <annotation ref="ps-box2b" text="Differentiating in y shows g does not depend on y, so f equals x y z plus h of z."/>
  <annotation ref="ps-box3" text="Step three. The z component of F says partial f partial z equals x y plus 2 z."/>
  <annotation ref="ps-box3b" text="Solving h prime of z equals 2 z gives h equals z squared plus C, so f equals x y z plus z squared plus C."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-conservative-gradient">
<title>Conservative Fields are Gradient Fields</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-conservative-gradient">
<title>Conservative Fields are Gradient Fields</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf F = M\mathbf i + N\mathbf j + P\mathbf k</m> be a vector
field whose components are continuous throughout an open connected
region <m>D</m> in space. Then <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative if and
only if <m>\mathbf F</m> is a gradient field <m>\nabla f</m> for a
differentiable function <m>f</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
The content of <xref ref="thm-conservative-gradient"/>, combined with
<xref ref="thm-ftli"/> and the definition of path independence, can be
organized into the following square of equivalent statements:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf F = \nabla f \text{ for some } f
\quad\amp\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\mathbf F \text{ is conservative}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\Big\Updownarrow\qquad\qquad\qquad \amp \qquad\qquad\qquad\Big\Updownarrow
</mrow>
<mrow>
\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = f(B) - f(A)
\quad\amp\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r \text{ is path-independent}
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Path independence means that all of the paths in
<xref ref="fig-path-independence-prefigure"/> give the same value of the
line integral: if <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative, then
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-three-paths">
\int_{C_1} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= \int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= \int_{C_3} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= f(B) - f(A).
</men>

<figure xml:id="fig-path-independence-prefigure">
<caption>
Three different paths <m>C_1</m>, <m>C_2</m>, and <m>C_3</m> joining
<m>A</m> to <m>B</m>. For a conservative field, all three line
integrals have the same value <m>f(B) - f(A)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-path-independence">
<diagram dimensions="(340,280)" margins="10">
<definition>c1(t) = (4*t - 1.6*t*(1-t), 3*t - 3.4*t*(1-t))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (4*t, 3*t)</definition>
<definition>c3(t) = (4*t - 3.4*t*(1-t), 3*t + 3.2*t*(1-t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-1.2,5.3,4.6)">
<parametric-curve at="pi-c1" function="c1" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="pi-c2" function="c2" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="pi-c3" function="c3" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<point at="pi-A" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>A</m>
</point>
<point at="pi-B" p="(4,3)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
<m>B</m>
</point>
<label at="pi-c1-label" anchor="(2.4,0.55)" alignment="se"
    color="#0072B2"><m>C_1</m></label>
<label at="pi-c2-label" anchor="(1.7,1.35)" alignment="nw"
    color="#009E73"><m>C_2</m></label>
<label at="pi-c3-label" anchor="(0.75,2.05)" alignment="nw"
    color="#D55E00"><m>C_3</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two points A and B in the plane joined by three different curves. C 1 bows below the straight path, C 2 is the straight segment from A to B, and C 3 bows above. Arrows on all three curves point from A to B.">
  <annotation ref="pi-A" text="The starting point A of all three paths."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-B" text="The ending point B of all three paths."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-c1" text="The path C 1, drawn in blue, bowing below the straight segment."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-c2" text="The path C 2, the straight segment from A to B, drawn in green."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-c3" text="The path C 3, drawn in vermillion, bowing above the straight segment."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-loop-property">
<title>The Loop Property of Conservative Fields</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-loop-property">
<title>Loop Property of Conservative Fields</title>
<statement>
<p>
The following statements are equivalent.
</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
<m>\oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = 0</m> around every loop (that
is, closed curve <m>C</m>) in <m>D</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The field <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative on <m>D</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>
We prove that (2) implies (1); the reverse direction runs the same
computation backwards. We assume that <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative.
Then, as shown in <xref ref="fig-loop-split-prefigure"/>, we can think
about any loop as <m>C = C_1 \cup C_2</m>, where <m>C_1</m> goes from
<m>A</m> to <m>B</m> and <m>C_2</m> returns from <m>B</m> to <m>A</m>.
Reversing the orientation of <m>C_2</m> produces the curve
<m>-C_2</m>, and <m>C_1</m> and <m>-C_2</m> both connect <m>A</m> to
<m>B</m>. Since <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative, path independence
gives
<m>\int_{C_1} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= \int_{-C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r</m>,
and therefore
</p>

<md xml:id="eq-loop-computation">
<mrow>
\oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
\amp= \int_{C_1} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
+ \int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_{-C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
+ \int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= -\int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
+ \int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 0.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Hence <m>\oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = 0</m> for any loop
<m>C</m>.
</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-loop-split-prefigure">
<caption>
On the left, a loop <m>C</m> is split into two pieces:
<m>C_1</m> from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> and <m>C_2</m> from <m>B</m> back
to <m>A</m>, so <m>C = C_1 \cup C_2</m>. On the right, the orientation
of <m>C_2</m> is reversed to produce <m>-C_2</m>; now <m>C_1</m> and
<m>-C_2</m> both connect <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>, and path independence
forces the two integrals to agree.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-loop-split">
<diagram dimensions="(300,260)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (2*cos(t), 1.35*sin(t) + 0.12*sin(2*t))</definition>
<definition>bot(t) = (2*cos(t), 1.35*sin(t) - 0.1*sin(2*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.4,3.1,2.4)">
<parametric-curve at="ls-c1" function="top" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="ls-c2" function="bot" domain="(pi,2*pi)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<point at="ls-A" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>A</m>
</point>
<point at="ls-B" p="(-2,0)" fill="black" alignment="w">
<m>B</m>
</point>
<label at="ls-c1-label" anchor="(-0.9,1.55)" alignment="n"
    color="#0072B2"><m>C_1</m></label>
<label at="ls-c2-label" anchor="(1.75,-1.05)" alignment="se"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>C_2</m></label>
<label at="ls-eq" anchor="(0,-1.85)" alignment="s">
<m>C = C_1 \cup C_2</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="An oval loop through the points A on the right and B on the left. The upper half, labeled C 1, is drawn in blue and runs counterclockwise from A to B. The lower half, labeled C 2, is drawn in reddish purple and continues from B back to A, closing the loop.">
  <annotation ref="ls-c1" text="The piece C 1 of the loop, running from A to B along the top."/>
  <annotation ref="ls-c2" text="The piece C 2 of the loop, running from B back to A along the bottom, so the loop is the union of C 1 and C 2."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-loop-reversed">
<diagram dimensions="(300,260)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (2*cos(t), 1.35*sin(t) + 0.12*sin(2*t))</definition>
<definition>botrev(t) = (2*cos(-t), 1.35*sin(-t) + 0.1*sin(2*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.4,3.1,2.4)">
<parametric-curve at="lr-c1" function="top" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="lr-negc2" function="botrev" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<point at="lr-A" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>A</m>
</point>
<point at="lr-B" p="(-2,0)" fill="black" alignment="w">
<m>B</m>
</point>
<label at="lr-c1-label" anchor="(-0.9,1.55)" alignment="n"
    color="#0072B2"><m>C_1</m></label>
<label at="lr-negc2-label" anchor="(1.75,-1.05)" alignment="se"
    color="#009E73"><m>-C_2</m></label>
<label at="lr-eq" anchor="(0,-1.85)" alignment="s">
<m>\int_{C_1}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= \int_{-C_2}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same oval through A and B, but now both halves run from A to B. The upper half C 1 is drawn in blue and the lower half, relabeled minus C 2 and drawn in green, has its arrow reversed so that it also runs from A to B. Since both curves connect A to B, path independence makes the two line integrals equal.">
  <annotation ref="lr-c1" text="The piece C 1, still running from A to B along the top."/>
  <annotation ref="lr-negc2" text="The reversed piece minus C 2, drawn in green, now running from A to B along the bottom. C 1 and minus C 2 both connect A to B."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- Questions 4-6 of the companion GeoGebra activity, with the three      -->
<!-- applets VF-fig 2, VF-fig 3 and VF-fig 4 (600 x 500).                  -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-conservative-concept-check">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
The three questions below all use the applets that go with them. In each
one, drag the slider to send the point along the curve and watch the field
vector and the tangent vector move with it; the sign of their dot product is
what the integral is accumulating. Every applet can be rotated by dragging
the background and zoomed by scrolling.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-vf-helix-work">
<title>Work Along the Helix, Two Ways</title>
<statement>
<p>
For the field and path of <xref ref="fig-vf-helix-work"/>:
</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
Use the parametrized path to compute the work done by the force field from
point <m>A</m> to point <m>B</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Is it possible to use the fundamental theorem of line integrals
<xref ref="eq-ftli"/> to compute that work? If so, use it to verify your
answer to the previous part. Justify your answer fully.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The work is <m>0</m>, by either method.
</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
<term>Directly.</term> On the helix
<m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle \cos t,\ \sin t,\ t/2\right\rangle</m> the
point <m>A=(1,0,0)</m> is <m>t=0</m> and <m>B=(1,0,\pi)</m> is
<m>t=2\pi</m>, since <m>z=t/2</m>. Along the curve
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf{F}\bigl(\mathbf{r}(t)\bigr) \amp
  = \left\langle -\cos t,\ \sin t,\ 0\right\rangle ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\mathbf{v}(t) \amp
  = \left\langle -\sin t,\ \cos t,\ \tfrac12\right\rangle ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{v} \amp
  = \cos t\sin t + \sin t\cos t = \sin(2t).
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Hence
</p>

<md>
W = \int_0^{2\pi}\sin(2t)\,dt
  = \left[-\tfrac12\cos(2t)\right]_0^{2\pi} = 0 .
</md>

<p>
<term>By the fundamental theorem.</term> Yes, it applies. The field
<m>\mathbf{F}=\left\langle -x,\ y,\ 0\right\rangle</m> is defined and
smooth on all of <m>\R^3</m>, which is open and simply connected, and its
mixed partials match:
<m>\partial(-x)/\partial y = 0 = \partial(y)/\partial x</m>, and the
<m>z</m>-component is <m>0</m> while neither of the others involves
<m>z</m>. So <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is conservative, with potential
</p>

<md>
f(x,y,z) = \frac{y^2-x^2}{2},
\qquad \nabla f = \left\langle -x,\ y,\ 0\right\rangle .
</md>

<p>
Therefore, by <xref ref="eq-ftli"/>,
</p>

<md>
W = f(B) - f(A) = f(1,0,\pi) - f(1,0,0)
  = \left(-\tfrac12\right) - \left(-\tfrac12\right) = 0 ,
</md>

<p>
which agrees with the direct computation. The reason is visible in the
picture: <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> differ only in <m>z</m>, and the potential
does not involve <m>z</m> at all, so climbing costs nothing. The work
gained on the stretches where <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> make
an acute angle is given back exactly on the stretches where the angle is
obtuse.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-vf-helix-work">
<caption>
A point climbing the helix
<m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle \cos t,\ \sin t,\ t/2\right\rangle</m> from
<m>A</m> to <m>B</m>, through the force field
<m>\mathbf{F}=\left\langle -x,\ y,\ 0\right\rangle</m>. The two arrows are
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> at the moving point and the velocity <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="arjpdtkt"
             width="100%" aspect="600:500"
             material-width="600" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider <m>t</m> to move the point from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> along
the helix. At each position the applet draws the force
<m>\mathbf{F}=\left\langle -x,y,0\right\rangle</m> acting there and the
velocity <m>\mathbf{v}=d\mathbf{r}/dt</m>. Watch the angle between them: it
is acute on some stretches and obtuse on others, so the work is being added
on some parts of the trip and taken back on others.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-vf-segment">
<title>A Line Integral Along a Segment</title>
<statement>
<p>
Compute the line integral of the vector field along the line segment shown
in <xref ref="fig-vf-segment"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
  = 1 + \frac{4\sqrt2}{3} \approx 2.886 .</m>
</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
The field <m>\mathbf{F}=\tfrac13\left\langle z,\ z,\ x+y\right\rangle</m> is
conservative: it is smooth on all of <m>\R^3</m> and
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{z}{3}\right) \amp = 0
  = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{z}{3}\right),
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial}{\partial z}\left(\frac{z}{3}\right) \amp = \frac13
  = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{x+y}{3}\right)
  = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{x+y}{3}\right).
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
A potential is found by inspection:
</p>

<md>
f(x,y,z) = \frac{(x+y)z}{3},
\qquad
\nabla f = \left\langle \frac{z}{3},\ \frac{z}{3},\
  \frac{x+y}{3}\right\rangle = \mathbf{F} .
</md>

<p>
So there is no need to parametrize the segment at all. By
<xref ref="eq-ftli"/>, with <m>A=(0,1,-1)</m> and
<m>B=(1,2\sqrt2,2)</m>,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} \amp = f(B)-f(A)
  = \frac{\left(1+2\sqrt2\right)(2)}{3} - \frac{(0+1)(-1)}{3}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \frac{2+4\sqrt2}{3} + \frac13
     = 1 + \frac{4\sqrt2}{3} \approx 2.886 .
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-vf-segment">
<caption>
The straight segment from <m>A=(0,1,-1)</m> to <m>B=(1,2\sqrt2,2)</m> in the
field <m>\mathbf{F}=\tfrac13\left\langle z,\ z,\ x+y\right\rangle</m>, with
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the unit tangent <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> drawn at the
moving point.
</caption>
<!-- Served from a local copy of the material whose stored window is
     458 + 142 = 600 wide by 500 high, so the narrow panel carrying the
     slider is part of the layout.  (The published material stores a
     1098 + 142 window, which loses the panel when squeezed to 600.) -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="600:500">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/vf-fig3-segment.ggb"
       aspect="600:500" material-width="600" material-height="500"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to move the point along the segment from <m>A</m> to
<m>B</m>. The applet draws the field vector <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the unit
tangent <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> at that point, so what the integral
accumulates is the length of the projection of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> onto
<m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m>. Rotate the view to see how the segment sits
relative to the field.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-vf-curve">
<title>The Same Endpoints, a Wilder Path</title>
<statement>
<p>
Compute the line integral of the vector field from point <m>A</m> to point
<m>B</m> as shown in <xref ref="fig-vf-curve"/>, where the curve is
parametrized as
</p>

<md>
\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle \frac{\ln(t)}{\ln(2)},\ t^{3/2},\
t\cos(\pi t)\right\rangle .
</md>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
  = 1 + \frac{4\sqrt2}{3} \approx 2.886</m>, exactly as in
<xref ref="q-vf-segment"/>.
</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
First check the endpoints. At <m>t=1</m>,
<m>\mathbf{r}(1)=\left\langle 0,\ 1,\ \cos\pi\right\rangle=(0,1,-1)=A</m>,
and at <m>t=2</m>,
<m>\mathbf{r}(2)=\left\langle 1,\ 2\sqrt2,\ 2\cos 2\pi\right\rangle
=(1,2\sqrt2,2)=B</m>. So this curve joins the same two points as the
segment of <xref ref="fig-vf-segment"/>.
</p>
<p>
Computing <m>\int_C\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> directly here would mean
differentiating <m>t\cos(\pi t)</m> and <m>t^{3/2}</m> and integrating the
resulting mess. There is no need: the field is the same conservative field
as before, with the same potential <m>f=(x+y)z/3</m>, so the integral
depends only on the endpoints:
</p>

<md>
\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = f(B) - f(A)
  = 1 + \frac{4\sqrt2}{3} \approx 2.886 .
</md>

<p>
This is the same number as in <xref ref="q-vf-segment"/>, and that is the
whole point: for a conservative field the path between two points is
irrelevant. A wildly complicated path and a straight segment cost exactly
the same.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-vf-curve">
<caption>
The same field <m>\mathbf{F}=\tfrac13\left\langle z,\ z,\ x+y\right\rangle</m>
and the same endpoints as in <xref ref="fig-vf-segment"/>, but now joined by
the curve
<m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle \frac{\ln t}{\ln 2},\ t^{3/2},\
t\cos(\pi t)\right\rangle</m>.
</caption>
<!-- as above: a local copy sized 459 + 141 by 500 -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="600:500">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/vf-fig4-curve.ggb"
       aspect="600:500" material-width="600" material-height="500"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to move the point along the curve from <m>A</m> to
<m>B</m>, watching <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the unit tangent
<m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> as before. Compare the shape of this path with the
segment of <xref ref="fig-vf-segment"/>: the two share their endpoints and
nothing else.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
